## Part 4: Why Complementary Masking Works

### Benefit 1: Covers All Positions

```
Example with 3 tokens [a, b, c]:

Forward path:  mask 50%  → [M, b, c]   (trains to predict 'a')
Rev path:      mask other → [a, M, c]  (trains to predict 'b')

With multiple iterations, EVERY position gets trained as both:
- A masked position to predict (learns to generate)
- A visible position for context (learns to understand)
```

### Benefit 2: Bidirectional Context

```
Predicting token at position i:

Forward path:   visible context = [visible_left] [MASK] [visible_right]
                Uses both left and right context!

Complementary:  visible context = [different_left] [MASK] [different_right]
                Different combination of left/right context!

Result: Model learns from DIVERSE context combinations
```

### Benefit 3: Robustness

```
At inference, model can handle:
- If some positions are masked
- If other positions are masked
- Any combination!

Because during training, it saw both scenarios for each token.
```

### Benefit 4: Data Efficiency

```
One input_ids sequence → Two training examples:
  Example 1: noisy_batch with masked_indices
  Example 2: noisy_batch_rev with masked_indices_rev

Doubles the effective training data! (Without storing extra data)
```

